title The guard that could not clear its own red
prompt The baton -- open the round, read the rota row, take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, reds first.
think Opened with fleet_round_open, clean at 2d1c434a50. Lap N is git rev-list --count HEAD = 4037, so 4037 mod 5 = 2, the FIRE row -- cut and stop, and its sense is vision. My last lap read row 1 by hand, so the commit count and the rota agreed for once. Fire's own threshold page says a lap reads the REDS ledger before it reads anything new, and looks at the thing most laps would rather route around. That is exactly what this lap turned out to be.
think Took the thread my own DEAD lap left. tools/f/fleet_round_open.sh had parked ten files at 20260906-015308 -- REDS %464's shape, a lap's whole work stashed and never read back. Its log recommended the four unrun tools/rye/mantra_a1_*_eq_witness.rye as the next lane door, wanting one measurement first. So the stash was read rather than left, which is the one thing %464 says nobody does.
think Measured before believing. Seven of the eight witnesses src/gate/README.md names when it declares the equality arc closed 8/8 have ZERO callers anywhere in tools/ or construction/; none of the eight stands on the roster; and glow_tend_a1_suite.rish, the suite the same page names one section down, is itself unrostered and names none of them. A sentence is not a runner.
think Then the cold pass closed and moved the whole lap. 129 green, 1 red, 4 gated, tree_moved=no -- and the one red was standing_equipment, the guard that reports whether the roster is honest. Reds first, so the equality work waited.
think Found the root by removing one row rather than by reasoning. The run card records a verdict per guard; the scan refuses on any recorded red. Copying the card, deleting the single row `ran standing_equipment ... red`, and re-running answered verdict=ok with nothing else changed. Every other reading was already clean.
think Read the runner to find WHY that row could exist. standing_equipment_run.sh writes the card AFTER every guard has run -- `sort "$pen/fresh" > "$card"` -- so a pass reads the PREVIOUS pass's card, and this scan is what decides the roster guard's own verdict. One red writes its own row, the next pass refuses on it and writes it again. The first cause need not still exist, and on this tree it did not.
think Chose exclude-by-name over the alternatives because the tree has already ruled this shape once. %463 excluded a census from its own corpus by name after it began crediting every path it mentioned. The runner already prints this guard's verdict on its own line and counts it in guards_red, so excluding the card ECHO hides nothing and is louder than the row it drops.
think Proved it from the failing side before trusting it, by copying HEAD's own scan into the fixtures room so it could resolve its sibling runner, and running the planted card through it: runs_red=1, red: standing_equipment red, verdict=roster_broken. The repaired scan on the same card: runs_red=0, runs_self_red=1, self_red named, verdict=ok. Then removed the probe.
think Wrote two anti-bypass phases on purpose. self_red_no_bypass plants a PEER's red in the same card and requires the refusal, and peer_red_still_counted requires runs_red=1 there. A refusal proven only in the passing direction cannot be told from a bypass, and an exclusion is exactly the kind of repair that becomes one.
think For the equality arc, built and ran all four on metal rather than reading them. All four compile and print GREEN -- 20s, 19s, 16s, 14s -- so they were unheard rather than rotted. Also walked every module in mantra/src/ with the comptime declaration walker and proved the walker bites by planting a type error in a pen copy: all five compile. grep proves a file's shape; only a compiler proves a file.
think Declined to copy the sibling guard's comptime walker into the new witness, and wrote down why. These four import no local Rye module -- their subject is a Glow desk read at run time, and each IS the root file -- so a walker would force nothing the build already forces. Copying it for symmetry would be a rite rather than a reading.
obs A GUARD WHOSE SUBJECT INCLUDES ITSELF CANNOT RECOVER ON ITS OWN. The reading was not wrong about the past; it was wrong about the present, and the only reading that could have gone green was the one being refused. Every repair anywhere else in the tree was powerless against it. This is the sharpest form of the shape %463 named and %467 named again: an instrument reading its own reflection and reporting the room.
obs BOTH OF THIS LAP'S REDS ARE ONE ROOT TWO ROOMS APART, which is why they share a shelf. The scan asked what did the card say last time where the claim is what is wrong now. The README asked do these witnesses exist where the claim is does anything run them. A cheap question and an expensive one can return the same word, and the cheap one returns it faster and more often.
obs AND THE QA CARD ASKS THE CHEAP QUESTION TOO, which is worth Keaton's eye. src/gate/README.md grades Truth at 100 on twelve resolving paths -- because Truth counts whether a cited path RESOLVES, and all eight witnesses are present. A page whose behavioral claim rested entirely on unrun proofs read B+/88 with a perfect Truth. That is %467's own sentence, and it now has a second instance in a different instrument.
obs THE PIN WAS 49 BYTES FROM ITS CEILING AND THE LEDGER 325. Folded only MY OWN two rows to their own shelf and condensed only MY OWN card block, touching no peer's paragraph -- because my last lap lost a fold to a peer's published fold, and the cheapest way not to repeat that is to move nothing that is not mine.
obs A NUMBER I TYPED BY HAND WAS FALSIFIED WITHIN THE LAP, again. I edited the ledger recital from 471 to 473 and reds_ledger_headline refused: the remainder and span move too. Its writer repaired all three. That is the third instance of the card's own spec -- a writer beside each guard -- and it cost one refusal rather than a rebase this time, because the guard ran before the send rather than after it.
obs THE LATCH EXPLAINS A FLEET-WIDE READING, not just mine. Any tree whose standing_equipment ever recorded a red has been reading roster_broken every pass since, whatever was repaired. %471 named two roster guards red on the incense tree and repaired the true cause; on any tree where the roster guard had already written its own row, that repair could not show.
